A||||11||JetroGT||Got my Lasik done!||||6||
Z||000000||JetroGT||01-27-2001||04:49 PM||||I did it!  I had the surgery done on Friday and today I was driving around without my glasses! It's still a little hazy. At 8 this morning I went for my followup. Right now I'm at about 20/25. Which is good concidering that it's my second day after the surgery. It was a two for one deal. I paid for mine and I could have a friends eyes done for free! I went first. They put me in the chair with a local already working. Then they put a few more numbing drops in. Taped my eyelid open and applied some kind of ring. On top of that they placed a another ring. This one was metal and was the one with the razor blade to cut my cornea. Talk about $hiting bricks! Then they applied suction to my eye and everything went black. That's when they cut my cornea back to blast it with the lazer. My left eye took 40 seconds of lazer, and my right only 20 seconds. I can see alot better now than I could with my glasses. It was a bit freeky, but worth it!||||reg||6
Z||000001||bHooper||01-27-2001||04:52 PM||||Dude!  Congrats!<P>I am blinder than the night (20/450 or so), plus i have astgmatism (cant even spell it).<P>so i am really jealous!<P>congrats!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||01-27-2001||04:58 PM||||Yeah but when those numbing drops wear off,<BR>AYE CARUMBA! <BR>Congrats! <BR>Had mine done a year ago in Canada. No regrets. ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Shaft||01-27-2001||07:19 PM||||Jetro,<P>My brother had that surgery about 3 weeks ago. His eyes were HORRIBLE before the surgery (any worse and he'd probably be considered legally blind and with astigmatism) and when he went for his follow up he found out he now has 20/25 vision also!! It's amazing and anyone with really bad eyes should go for it. What do you have to lose.<p>[This message has been edited by Shaft (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||Eric||01-28-2001||12:41 AM||||I think I'm about 20/600, give or take a few hundred feet.  I was told I shouldn't go under the laser till I'm about 24, so as to give my eyes time enough to adjust to my constantly expanding brain!  Seriously, I'm probably legally blind all the time plus I'm blind drunk most of the time.  I really just type by feel.  It's getting kind of late; I've gotta fly a plane tommorow  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||01-28-2001||02:08 AM||||I was thinking about getting that done, but my parents are telling me not too. I heard that you can end up going back to, or worse, than before, and it usually does when you hit 40 or something? Someone give me the lowdown on this, would ya?||||reg||1
Z||000006||jlhuber||01-28-2001||03:15 AM||||When I went to Lens Crafters last year to get new glasses, I asked the Doc there about the laser deal.  He also mentioned the 24 year thing.  I found out recently after I talked with the Doc, that the U.S. Air Force is doing a study on the laser surgery.  From what I hear, it will first be offered to pilots and those mission essential personnel.  After that, the rest of the USAF population can recieve it is they wish.  It is covered by the free medical section of the military contract too.  Kinda makes me not want to get out if I can get it for free.  Course, it is still being studied right now, no word on if/when they are actually going to approve it.<P>--Jess<p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JetroGT||01-28-2001||04:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I was thinking about getting that done, but my parents are telling me not too. I heard that you can end up going back to, or worse, than before, and it usually does when you hit 40 or something? Someone give me the lowdown on this, would ya?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You will need reading glasses when you get to be around 40. Most people that have 20/20 vision now will also have to get reading glasses. My glasses have had the same prescripton for the past 4 years. I figured, what the heck. The doctor will also tell you not to get it done if your prescription is not stable. Getting Lasik dosen't mean that you won't have to wear glasses any more. But it will greatly improve your vision. Just don't rub your eyes after the surgery. Your cornea might fall off! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Scott_M||01-29-2001||01:29 AM||||I had it done April 99'. It's been great!!!! My glasses were so think I could almost see into the future. Now I have 20/20. I feel like a normal human being.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Signature.jpg">   <BR>87 SE 4cyl,5 spd. manual||||reg||10
Z||000009||flyntus||01-29-2001||08:11 PM||||I had the lasik surgery done back in Aug 00 for my 49th birthday.  I had been wearing glasses since I was about 7 years old. Until the lasik surgery, I didn't know roads had curbs at night. I hated to drive at night in the rain. Now the roads are so much clearer at night time.  I still have to wear reading glasses for anything like reading or looking at my pc monitor. (Roughly anything closer than 3 ft).  My pool game has improved a lot especially on the long shots.  I'm glad I got it done, but getting old is a PAIN!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||01-29-2001||08:22 PM||||Anyone had problems with night vision??? it is suppose to affect your vision at night in some cases.<P>quote:<BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR>I was thinking about getting that done, but my parents are telling me not too. I heard that you can end up going back to, or worse, than before, and it usually does when you hit 40 or something? Someone give me the lowdown on this, would ya?<BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>your eyes can potentially go back , but CAN'T get worse then they already were. <P>Joanthan M McCreery<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||frontal lobe||01-30-2001||12:59 AM||||OK, how many fingers am I holding up?? j/k<P>Here's a little of the scoop:<P>Once you get over 40ish, even if you have 20/20 vision, your lens (not the cornea, which is where the lasik is done) INSIDE your eye gets stiff and can't adjust as well, so you can't focus up close.  I'm trying to get adjusted to that now, and it is A PAIN.  So if lasik gets you to 20/20, you won't need bifocals, but you still would need the "granny glasses" for up close.<P>Regarding the night vision, your pupil opens wider in the dark and may allow light into your eye that is passing over the teeny scar tissue where the cornea was cut.  This scar can slightly scatter light rays and cause you to see a "star-burst" effect.<P>What the air force is studying is what is the complication rate of the procedure.  It's hard to know what the long-term complications are when it hasn't been around "long-term" yet.<P>If anyone wants to scare themselves out of having it done, go to <A HREF="http://www.surgicaleyes.com," TARGET=_blank>www.surgicaleyes.com,</A>  which is a site for people that have had complications to go and tell what their (bad) experiences are.<P>I'm not trying to dissuade anyone from getting it.  Just throwing out some facts.  I'd like to get it myself.  Hmmm.  V8 conversion or lasik.....||||reg||1
